Cloudberry's singlenode tests on the port: the differences from Cloudberry's
expected output that have been reviewed, and are the port's for a reason of
its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../canon.pl gives it -- the
lines removed and added, hunk by hunk, with no file names, line numbers or
plan lines -- named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a
pass in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  run.sh counts a test
as passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file and the port's, finds
no difference or exactly the file's.

Most of every difference is a statement that fails, for a reason its error
names, and what comes of it: the rows a query over a table that was never
made would have returned, a later statement in the same transaction refused.
The reasons, by what brings them, and then each test:

  Append-optimized and column-oriented tables, their catalogs and the bitmap
  index -- WITH (appendonly=true), orientation, ENCODING, compresstype,
  pg_appendonly, pg_attribute_encoding, USING bitmap, ao_row: M5.

  External tables, gpfdist, COPY ... LOG ERRORS and the error log functions,
  FILL MISSING FIELDS, NEWLINE, ESCAPE outside CSV mode: M5, the loading path.

  Cloudberry's catalogs are the port's since M2, by their names, in
  pg_catalog: gp_id, gp_segment_configuration, which lists the one node,
  gp_configuration_history, written only with allow_system_table_mods, and
  gp_distribution_policy, which on one node has no row, as Cloudberry's
  single node keeps no policy (gp_catalog.c).  test_print_prefetch_joinqual
  is accepted and prints nothing: PostgreSQL's joins never prefetch their
  quals.

  Cloudberry's settings of the dispatcher and the planner are the port's
  since M2 (gp_settings.c), respelled gp.* as every one is, and a test that
  sets one runs on; on one node test_print_direct_dispatch_info prints
  nothing, as Cloudberry's single-node mode does not.

  Cloudberry's table functions, TABLE (...) and anytable: grammar the port's
  O26 does not have, and a pseudo-type an extension cannot add.

  Cloudberry's classic partition syntax, which O26 takes since M2's first step,
  where the port and Cloudberry still part:
    - ADD, DROP, ALTER and RENAME PARTITION can be PostgreSQL's too, about a
      column called "partition", which PostgreSQL 19 lets be a name and
      Cloudberry reserves.  Where PostgreSQL's grammar takes the statement
      the port leaves it PostgreSQL's, so ALTER TABLE t DROP partition is
      "column "partition" of relation "t" does not exist" where Cloudberry's
      is a syntax error, and ADD partition cc a column of type cc where
      Cloudberry's is a partition with no boundary.  Both fail in Cloudberry.
    - A SUBPARTITION TEMPLATE is kept, as written, in its table's gp label;
      Cloudberry's catalog gp_partition_template, and the node dump its
      tests print from it, is not there to query.
    - An error about a partition an ALTER TABLE made is reported by
      Cloudberry at the 35th character of the statement, the length of the
      text it runs the partition's CREATE as, "(internally generated SQL
      command)"; the port reports it at the value it is about.
  Cloudberry's REVOKE says "no privileges could be revoked" when it revokes
  nothing, which PostgreSQL's does not, on any object.

  gp_recursive_cte: a setting the port has no counterpart of.  Recursive CTEs
  are always on, as in PostgreSQL.

  What Cloudberry's tests make with PL/Python -- count_operator(),
  find_operator(), qx_count_operator() -- counts the nodes of a plan, and so
  makes a plan an answer.  The expected output is Cloudberry's single-node
  planner's: under the port's planner a count differs where PostgreSQL 19
  plans otherwise, and under ORCA where ORCA's plan has nodes of its own --
  Dynamic Seq Scan, Dynamic Index Scan, Partition Selector -- or joins or
  aggregates otherwise.  Such a test keeps a difference for each pass.
  qx_count_operator() chooses which node to count by "Pivotal Optimizer
  (GPORCA)" in the plan, which neither Cloudberry's EXPLAIN nor the port's
  prints now ("Optimizer: GPORCA"), so under ORCA it counts the planner's
  node in ORCA's plan.

  PostgreSQL 19's own behaviour where Cloudberry changed its parser or
  PostgreSQL has changed since: OVER (w) of a window with a frame ("cannot
  copy window"), a frame offset that reads a column ("argument of ROWS must
  not contain variables"), a chain of UNIONs over NULLs, which PostgreSQL
  types one UNION at a time ("UNION types text and integer cannot be
  matched"), IS NOT DISTINCT FROM printed as NOT ... IS DISTINCT FROM,
  standard_conforming_strings = off and escape_string_warning, which
  PostgreSQL 19 no longer has, NOT NULL constraints, which PostgreSQL 18
  keeps in pg_constraint, and "operator does not exist", which PostgreSQL 19
  explains in a DETAIL and a HINT where PostgreSQL 14 had one HINT.  And of
  partitioned tables: none may be UNLOGGED ("partitioned tables cannot be
  unlogged"), an exclusion constraint on one is allowed (PostgreSQL 17),
  "UNIQUE constraint" or "PRIMARY KEY constraint on partitioned table must
  include all partitioning columns" names the constraint's kind, and
  "cannot alter column ... because it is part of the partition key" has a
  caret; PUBLIC may no longer CREATE in schema public (PostgreSQL 15), so a
  table a role of the test's makes there is refused; and psql's \d+ lists
  each partition on a line of its own, under "Partitions:".

  DECODE and CASE x WHEN IS NOT DISTINCT FROM y, which Cloudberry's parser
  makes a CASE with an operand and the port's rewrite a searched CASE with
  the operand in each arm, because PostgreSQL 19 compares a CASE's operand
  with = and nothing else: a view prints the searched CASE, and the operand
  is evaluated in each arm the CASE tries rather than once.  The answers are
  Cloudberry's, and so are the errors and where they are reported.

The tests

bfv_aggregate (planner, orca)  an append-optimized table, OVER (w) with a
  frame, a frame offset that reads a column; and under ORCA the
  GroupAggregate and HashAggregate count_operator() counts, which ORCA
  chooses otherwise.
bfv_catalog  pg_stat_last_operation (Cloudberry's catalog of when an object
  was last changed), escape_string_warning and standard_conforming_strings =
  off, and one row more in a query over pg_constraint: the table's NOT NULL
  constraint.
bfv_dd  the bitmap index.
bfv_index  append-optimized and column-oriented tables, whose compression
  options a partitioned table is refused for before its access method.
bfv_partition_plans (planner, orca)  append-optimized and column-oriented
  tables and the bitmap index, and a plan gpdiff.pl does not take for one
  after the column-oriented table's statements fail; and under ORCA the
  Dynamic Seq Scans, Dynamic Index Scans and Partition Selectors that
  count_operator() and find_operator() find, where Cloudberry's planner makes
  none.
bfv_olap  OVER (w) with a frame.
case_gp  The deparse of IS NOT DISTINCT FROM, and of the CASE form, which
  prints as the searched CASE it was rewritten into; blip(1), the operand of
  a CASE with three arms, called three times where Cloudberry calls it once,
  so calls_to_blip has seven rows where it has five and the CASE's answer is
  the same 666; and PostgreSQL 19's DETAIL for "operator does not exist",
  twice.
cluster_gp  an append-optimized table.
create_table_like_gp  external tables, append-optimized tables and their
  catalogs, ENCODING, gp_default_storage_options, ao_row.
gp_gin_index  the complex type, which Cloudberry's own earlier tests make;
  and the index wowidx and the rows of test_tsvector that PostgreSQL 19's
  tsearch test, which runs before it here, leaves behind (two counts one and
  three higher).
gp_recursive_cte  gp_recursive_cte, whose "off" Cloudberry's test expects to
  refuse a recursive CTE, and a plan gpdiff.pl does not take for one, which is
  PostgreSQL 19's planner's.
gpcopy  COPY's LOG ERRORS, FILL MISSING FIELDS, NEWLINE and ESCAPE outside
  CSV mode, the error log functions, and the broken_int4 type of
  Cloudberry's regress.so.
indexjoin  an append-optimized table.
partition  Every reason above: its templates and gp_partition_template;
  append-optimized and column-oriented tables, column ENCODING, external
  tables and COPY ... LOG ERRORS; a role that may not CREATE in schema public;
  Cloudberry's REVOKE notice; an exclusion constraint PostgreSQL 19 allows;
  the caret of an error in a partition an ALTER TABLE made; psql's \d+.
partition1  append-optimized tables -- hhh and foz are, and most of its
  ALTER TABLE cases are about hhh; PostgreSQL's reading of ALTER TABLE ...
  DROP partition; the wording and caret of PostgreSQL 19's partition key
  errors; the bitmap index; psql's \d+.
partition_ddl  append-optimized tables and their options; "operator does
  not exist" explained in a DETAIL.
partition_indexing  the bitmap index, and "UNIQUE constraint on partitioned
  table".
partition_unlogged  an unlogged partitioned table, which PostgreSQL 19 has
  none of.
qp_query_execution (planner, orca)  a column-oriented table; and under ORCA
  qx_count_operator() of its hash joins and nested loops, which ORCA plans
  otherwise, counted by the planner's name for each (see above).
qp_with_clause  append-optimized and column-oriented tables, the bitmap
  index.
returning_gp  an append-optimized table.
singlenode_compatibility_cbdb  the warning Cloudberry's single-node mode
  gives for DISTRIBUTED BY (the port records the policy, for ORCA and M2),
  append-optimized tables, and TABLE (...) and anytable.  ALTER TABLE ... SET
  DISTRIBUTED BY is refused as Cloudberry refuses it in single-node mode.
subselect_gp  append-optimized tables.
subselect_gp2  an external web table.
tag  Tag definitions are the database's here, in gp_sql.tag, and Cloudberry's
  are the cluster's, in the shared catalog pg_tag (cloudberry.md, "Still
  open"): the test makes its tags in one database and uses them from postgres
  and other_db, where the port has none.  Its queries of pg_tag and
  pg_tag_description find no such catalogs.  CREATE TAG, ALTER TAG and DROP
  TAG are CALLs of gp_sql's procedures, and an error from one is Cloudberry's
  message, with a CONTEXT naming gp_sql's functions.  Where gp_sql is not
  there -- in the two databases the tags are not defined in -- a TAG clause is
  refused before its statement runs, "tags need the gp_sql extension in this
  database", and ALTER SCHEMA ... TAG, which is a CALL, finds no schema
  gp_sql, reported at the start of the statement.  A malformed TAG clause
  (TAG; or TAG ()) is PostgreSQL's error at the word the rewrite left -- a
  syntax error, or "unrecognized role option" of a role's -- where
  Cloudberry's syntax error is at the next.
union_gp (planner, orca)  an external table, and a chain of UNIONs over
  NULLs; and under the planner count_operator() of an Append, one where
  Cloudberry's planner has two: PostgreSQL 19 pulls a WITH query's UNION ALL
  up into the UNION ALL that reads it, and ORCA, like Cloudberry, does not.
update_gp  append-optimized tables.
